{"slug":"reverse-array","title":"Reverse an Array","topic":"basics","difficulty":"easy","order":49,"tags":["arrays","loops","indexes"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"Walking an array backwards","body":["You can visit the positions of an array in any order. To go from the last element to the first, start the index at `n - 1` and count down to 0 (inclusive). Getting the first and last index right is the whole difficulty: the last valid index is `n - 1`, not `n`.",{"code":"# Python\nfor i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):      # n-1, n-2, ..., 0\n    print(a[i], end=\" \")\n\n// C++ and Java\nfor (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    ...\n}"},"Reading `a[n]` is an error: C++ may silently read garbage, Java throws an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` and Python raises an `IndexError`. This kind of mistake is called an *out-of-bounds* access.","In Python, `print(x, end=\" \")` prints without starting a new line, so several values end up on the same line."]},"statement":["You are given `n` integers. Print them in reverse order, separated by spaces, on one line."],"inputFormat":["The first line has `n`. The second line has `n` integers."],"outputFormat":["The `n` integers in reverse order."],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 1000","-10^9 <= each number <= 10^9"],"examples":[{"input":"4\n1 2 3 4\n","output":"4 3 2 1\n","note":"Output: 4 3 2 1."},{"input":"1\n9\n","output":"9\n","note":"One element stays as it is."}],"hints":["The last element is at index `n - 1`, the first at index 0.","Use a loop where the index starts at `n - 1` and goes down to 0.","Print each element followed by a space (in Python add `end=\" \"` to the print)."],"starter":{"python":"n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Your code here\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cin >> a[i];\n\n    // Your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n        long[] a = new long[(int) n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) a[i] = sc.nextLong();\n\n        // Your code here\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["The index starts at the last position and moves left until it passes 0, printing every element on the way. Trailing spaces do not matter to the judge."],"complexity":"Time O(n), space O(1) besides the input.","code":{"python":"n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\nfor i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n    print(a[i], end=\" \")\nprint()\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cin >> a[i];\n\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        cout << a[i] << \" \";\n    }\n    cout << endl;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n        long[] a = new long[(int) n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) a[i] = sc.nextLong();\n\n        for (int i = (int) n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            System.out.print(a[i] + \" \");\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"4\n1 2 3 4\n","output":"4 3 2 1\n"},{"input":"1\n9\n","output":"9\n"},{"input":"2\n1 2\n","output":"2 1\n"},{"input":"3\n-1 0 1\n","output":"1 0 -1\n"},{"input":"3\n5 5 5\n","output":"5 5 5\n"},{"input":"1000\n-252077282 210582541 555373738 -772440398 18194514 -582552225 -132748416 720574031 -960112500 -663061547 367975880 325536520 103079877 -489679982 -274401600 727935354 -240981466 -162514086 -363003250 703983194 299536199 925757729 -84220415 -36568313 88066860 769684482 103517629 -323742537 -42725